WEBVTT

1
00:00:48,726 --> 00:00:50,060
- Whoa!
- Yeah!

2
00:00:50,061 --> 00:00:52,558
Dad,

3
00:00:52,560 --> 00:00:53,060
- Go!
- Get him!

4
00:00:56,118 --> 00:00:57,499
now you're going to crush them.

5
00:01:02,073 --> 00:01:05,200
- This is the best day!
- Hooray! Yeah!

6
00:01:14,078 --> 00:01:19,757
Happy birthday, Zuzanka.

7
00:01:21,123 --> 00:01:25,237
Come on, let's take a photo.

8
00:01:26,298 --> 00:01:27,938
Come closer together.

9
00:01:28,798 --> 00:01:31,158
There we go.

10
00:01:32,697 --> 00:01:34,558
Ready, set, go.

11
00:01:35,878 --> 00:01:36,718
I'm coming.

12
00:01:58,819 --> 00:02:00,819
Grandma, can you tell us your story?

13
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:04,060
I'd love to. Come here, kids.
You know the drill.

14
00:02:04,319 --> 00:02:07,799
Three ingredients, no more, no less.

15
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:09,680
I'll pick a match.

16
00:02:10,840 --> 00:02:13,560
And I'll pick an old gardener,
like Grandpa.

17
00:02:14,760 --> 00:02:15,200
Wow.

18
00:02:15,961 --> 00:02:17,901
Derek, what about the third one?

19
00:02:18,700 --> 00:02:21,401
I don't know. A magic photo?

20
00:02:21,860 --> 00:02:24,501
Hey, wait for me, I'm coming.

21
00:02:24,961 --> 00:02:25,540
Okay.

22
00:02:28,621 --> 00:02:31,621
One,

23
00:02:33,140 --> 00:02:33,820
two, three.

24
00:02:35,401 --> 00:02:36,721
Once upon a time...

25
00:02:50,856 --> 00:02:51,477
And here we are.

26
00:02:52,556 --> 00:02:54,456
I had a dream about Grandma.

27
00:02:55,417 --> 00:02:56,836
I miss Grandma too.

28
00:02:57,616 --> 00:02:59,657
All right. I'm counting on you.

29
00:03:00,157 --> 00:03:01,537
Be good for Grandpa.

30
00:03:01,877 --> 00:03:03,336
It's not easy for him.

31
00:03:06,516 --> 00:03:07,417
Hurry up!

32
00:03:10,836 --> 00:03:15,937
Grandpa, Grandpa!

33
00:03:41,752 --> 00:03:43,192
It's us.

34
00:03:46,092 --> 00:03:49,252
You're here already.

35
00:03:50,092 --> 00:03:50,972
My dear.

36
00:03:51,831 --> 00:03:53,954
- Hi.
- Look.

37
00:03:55,912 --> 00:04:00,331
You've all grown so much.

38
00:04:01,331 --> 00:04:04,271
Hi.

39
00:04:04,752 --> 00:04:05,352
Hi.

40
00:04:06,451 --> 00:04:06,991
Derek.

41
00:04:08,032 --> 00:04:09,431
Let it be.

42
00:04:11,013 --> 00:04:13,013
Hi, Dad.

43
00:04:14,152 --> 00:04:16,059
Grandma, hello?

44
00:04:17,213 --> 00:04:17,833
Grandma?

45
00:04:18,692 --> 00:04:19,632
Hello?

46
00:04:20,992 --> 00:04:22,392
Where's Grandma?

47
00:04:39,581 --> 00:04:41,141
Tom, remember.

48
00:04:42,281 --> 00:04:43,262
We've already talked about this.

49
00:04:44,302 --> 00:04:46,182
Grandma isn't here anymore.

50
00:05:29,870 --> 00:05:31,529
Sorry, this is no place for kids.

51
00:05:32,189 --> 00:05:34,209
Derek, Suzie!

52
00:05:35,589 --> 00:05:37,509
Kids, I have to go now.

53
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:39,189
Be good for Grandpa.

54
00:05:40,850 --> 00:05:41,569
Bye.

55
00:05:41,769 --> 00:05:42,670
Goodbye.

56
00:05:43,129 --> 00:05:43,629
Bye.

57
00:05:46,192 --> 00:05:47,490
Kids,

58
00:05:50,310 --> 00:05:51,750
off to bed.

59
00:05:55,637 --> 00:05:58,898
Grandpa, will you tell us a bedtime story?

60
00:05:58,997 --> 00:05:59,838
A story?

61
00:06:00,398 --> 00:06:02,798
Not tonight, sweetheart. It's late.

62
00:06:42,685 --> 00:06:45,685
Just

63
00:06:55,264 --> 00:06:55,865
stop it!

64
00:06:57,925 --> 00:07:00,125
That's enough!

65
00:07:00,444 --> 00:07:01,485
You'd better go to sleep.

66
00:07:03,404 --> 00:07:04,944
Can I sleep with you?

67
00:07:05,305 --> 00:07:07,165
Stay in your own bed.

68
00:07:07,925 --> 00:07:10,404
But I can't sleep without a story.

69
00:07:23,641 --> 00:07:25,901
What are you doing with Grandma's hat?

70
00:07:29,261 --> 00:07:30,521
Three ingredients.

71
00:07:31,081 --> 00:07:33,040
Not one more, not one less.

72
00:07:33,620 --> 00:07:35,620
Stop it. Only Grandma could do that.

73
00:07:36,380 --> 00:07:41,980
An umbrella, a kitten, and
strawberry cake. And the police.

74
00:07:44,480 --> 00:07:45,980
You've got one too many.

75
00:07:46,440 --> 00:07:48,060
Fine, no cake then.

76
00:07:52,024 --> 00:07:53,884
One, two, three.

77
00:07:54,605 --> 00:07:58,024
Once upon a time, there was a big
sister and a little brother.

78
00:07:58,045 --> 00:08:00,504
No way. Why is the brother
always the little one?

79
00:08:01,024 --> 00:08:02,464
All right, then.

80
00:08:03,064 --> 00:08:05,485
No, you'd better just go to sleep.

81
00:08:15,194 --> 00:08:17,733
Once upon a time, there was a brother
and his little sister.

82
00:08:18,374 --> 00:08:23,494
They lived in a city where it rained
almost all the time.

83
00:08:23,634 --> 00:08:26,334
Promise it won't be a sad story?

84
00:08:26,713 --> 00:08:31,213
I can't promise that. But I promise
a happy ending, okay?

85
00:08:31,733 --> 00:08:33,833
Standa, wait! I'm getting wet.

86
00:08:34,114 --> 00:08:35,433
Don't dawdle.

87
00:08:35,734 --> 00:08:37,854
Give me the umbrella. I'd better carry it.

88
00:08:40,754 --> 00:08:44,374
Hold it steady!

89
00:09:00,756 --> 00:09:01,917
Emilka, where are you going?

90
00:09:04,236 --> 00:09:06,956
Shhh, don't scare her off.

91
00:09:07,756 --> 00:09:09,177
She must have wandered off.

92
00:09:09,917 --> 00:09:11,437
A cat getting lost?

93
00:09:12,076 --> 00:09:13,297
More like she was abandoned.

94
00:09:16,956 --> 00:09:23,537
Don't worry, we'll look after you.

95
00:09:27,436 --> 00:09:29,135
You're so friendly.

96
00:09:29,615 --> 00:09:30,895
You aren't scared at all, are you?

97
00:09:31,816 --> 00:09:33,956
I know! We'll call you Fearless.

98
00:09:35,255 --> 00:09:41,696
Hello.

99
00:09:41,875 --> 00:09:43,535
What's that thing you've brought in?

100
00:09:43,615 --> 00:09:46,056
This is our cat. Her name's Fearless.

101
00:09:46,816 --> 00:09:48,956
Gross. She probably has fleas.

102
00:09:49,956 --> 00:09:50,956
Nasty thing.

103
00:09:58,773 --> 00:10:00,133
What have you brought home now?

104
00:10:00,793 --> 00:10:01,332
A cat?

105
00:10:01,893 --> 00:10:03,793
Just when your mother and I
are going to the

106
00:10:03,794 --> 00:10:05,653
theater. And who's going to
look after her?

107
00:10:05,773 --> 00:10:06,692
Both of us.

108
00:10:07,092 --> 00:10:08,653
Right, you two. We've heard that before.

109
00:10:09,092 --> 00:10:12,572
But we couldn't just leave
Fearless in the rain.

110
00:10:13,253 --> 00:10:15,212
She was soaking wet and hungry.

111
00:10:15,613 --> 00:10:16,033
Who?

112
00:10:16,273 --> 00:10:17,092
Fearless!

113
00:10:17,472 --> 00:10:19,232
Yes, that's what we named her.

114
00:10:19,413 --> 00:10:21,993
Karel, please get dressed, or
we'll be late!

115
00:10:31,274 --> 00:10:32,673
Your mother and I have to go now.

116
00:10:33,374 --> 00:10:37,214
The cat can stay until morning,

117
00:10:37,413 --> 00:10:39,774
but then we'll decide what to do with her.

118
00:10:40,014 --> 00:10:43,494
At least we won't be all alone
while you're out.

119
00:10:43,913 --> 00:10:46,014
And definitely keep her off the beds.

120
00:10:46,313 --> 00:10:47,374
Of course.

121
00:10:47,854 --> 00:10:51,553
Honey, should I wear the red tie
or the blue one?

122
00:10:51,894 --> 00:10:53,634
The blue one, it suits you better, Karel.

123
00:10:54,433 --> 00:10:56,254
And please, where are my...

124
00:10:56,254 --> 00:10:58,234
Your cufflinks are under the mirror.

125
00:10:59,114 --> 00:10:59,333
No.

126
00:11:00,907 --> 00:11:02,586
Just don't forget the tickets.

127
00:11:03,346 --> 00:11:05,047
Right, the tickets.

128
00:11:05,466 --> 00:11:07,547
Tickets, tickets.

129
00:11:08,106 --> 00:11:10,206
Where did I put them?

130
00:11:12,407 --> 00:11:14,947
Kids, whoever finds them gets
some chocolate.

131
00:11:18,966 --> 00:11:21,966
Dad...

132
00:11:26,447 --> 00:11:27,567
Look, here they are.

133
00:11:28,631 --> 00:11:31,292
Of course, on the chair. I might've known.

134
00:11:31,412 --> 00:11:33,672
Well done, Emilka. I owe you
some chocolate.

135
00:11:34,432 --> 00:11:37,272
But Nebojsa found them for you, Dad.

136
00:11:37,532 --> 00:11:43,011
Right. Well, then Nebojsa
gets the chocolate.

137
00:11:43,451 --> 00:11:45,192
We should keep Nebojsa.

138
00:11:47,052 --> 00:11:49,991
Whenever you lose something,
he'll find it.

139
00:11:50,412 --> 00:11:52,432
Emilka, Dad and I have to go now.

140
00:11:52,731 --> 00:11:53,951
Off you go, both of you, to bed.

141
00:12:01,419 --> 00:12:02,559
This is for the tickets.

142
00:12:04,840 --> 00:12:07,840
Good

143
00:12:12,039 --> 00:12:12,720
night, Emilka.

144
00:12:13,159 --> 00:12:14,080
Sleep tight.

145
00:12:14,279 --> 00:12:14,980
Yes, Mom.

146
00:12:15,460 --> 00:12:16,379
Goodnight, Standa.

147
00:12:17,299 --> 00:12:19,360
And don't read for too long.

148
00:12:19,820 --> 00:12:23,299
I know, but it's just so exciting.

149
00:12:23,799 --> 00:12:26,259
Mom, will you play something else for us?

150
00:12:26,580 --> 00:12:27,860
Please, please?

151
00:12:28,179 --> 00:12:30,620
All right, but just a little bit.

152
00:12:32,065 --> 00:12:36,144
Wouldn't you rather have a scary story?

153
00:12:36,605 --> 00:12:37,024
Mom!

154
00:12:37,865 --> 00:12:40,585
Standa, don't you dare scare Emilka.

155
00:12:41,184 --> 00:12:41,605
Do you hear me?

156
00:12:42,764 --> 00:12:43,284
I know.

157
00:13:24,832 --> 00:13:27,832
Good

158
00:13:35,913 --> 00:13:36,592
night, kids.

159
00:13:59,409 --> 00:14:02,409
Looks like someone's here...

160
00:14:23,288 --> 00:14:24,408
Who could that be?

161
00:14:24,947 --> 00:14:27,707
Who do you think? They probably
forgot something.

162
00:14:28,347 --> 00:14:29,389
Go and open it.

163
00:14:29,447 --> 00:14:30,148
I'm reading.

164
00:14:30,508 --> 00:14:32,248
But I'm too scared to go alone.

165
00:14:33,268 --> 00:14:37,508
Come on then, you scaredy-cat.

166
00:14:38,947 --> 00:14:42,788
Standa, wait for me! Don't run off!

167
00:14:43,187 --> 00:14:44,447
Lagging behind again?

168
00:14:46,148 --> 00:14:49,548
Standa, Dad clearly told you
not to scare me.

169
00:14:54,389 --> 00:14:54,869
Standa?

170
00:14:56,528 --> 00:14:57,788
Where are you?

171
00:14:58,849 --> 00:15:01,609
I'm waiting for you by the door.

172
00:15:03,288 --> 00:15:05,629
So, what did you forget?

173
00:15:06,249 --> 00:15:08,928
Dad, Standa's been scaring me
the whole time.

174
00:15:10,649 --> 00:15:13,649
Children,

175
00:15:14,869 --> 00:15:17,828
it's a terrible situation, such a tragedy.

176
00:15:18,208 --> 00:15:19,589
What will you do now?

177
00:15:26,485 --> 00:15:29,605
Come now, children.

178
00:15:31,465 --> 00:15:35,164
I'll tell you then.

179
00:15:35,544 --> 00:15:42,085
Since we're on duty today, it's our sad
duty to inform you that as your

180
00:15:42,085 --> 00:15:48,625
parents left the house, a speeding truck
happened to be passing by.

181
00:15:49,638 --> 00:15:52,812
It skidded, mounted the curb,
and sadly, your

182
00:15:52,813 --> 00:15:55,918
parents couldn't get out of
the way in time.

183
00:15:56,557 --> 00:16:01,457
They had to go to the hospital and will
be there for several weeks to recover.

184
00:16:03,577 --> 00:16:10,278
We immediately started searching
to see if anyone

185
00:16:10,278 --> 00:16:11,677
would be willing to take you in.

186
00:16:12,677 --> 00:16:14,778
After all, you're still just
children, aren't you?

187
00:16:16,258 --> 00:16:18,677
But unfortunately, we haven't found

188
00:16:19,725 --> 00:16:21,306
anyone to take you.

189
00:16:22,645 --> 00:16:23,725
That's just the way it is.

190
00:16:24,625 --> 00:16:31,186
If no one turns up by tomorrow, we'll
have to take you to a children's home.

191
00:16:31,546 --> 00:16:34,905
To a children's home?

192
00:16:35,166 --> 00:16:37,125
They'll know how to look after you there.

193
00:16:37,405 --> 00:16:39,426
You poor little things.

194
00:16:39,806 --> 00:16:41,326
You have no one now.

195
00:16:42,546 --> 00:16:46,365
Whoa! You gave me a fright.

196
00:16:46,591 --> 00:16:49,671
Well, what about you, neighbor?

197
00:16:50,211 --> 00:16:53,971
No, no, I'm sorry, but I don't have the
patience for children like these.

198
00:16:55,551 --> 00:16:58,551
Well,

199
00:17:01,150 --> 00:17:06,830
children, we've told you everything we
were required to say.

200
00:17:08,631 --> 00:17:15,330
We wish we had better news for
you, but unfortunately

201
00:17:15,330 --> 00:17:16,230
we don't.

202
00:17:56,503 --> 00:17:59,503
Thank you

203
00:18:26,403 --> 00:18:26,483
for your attention!

204
00:18:30,253 --> 00:18:33,253
Thank you.

205
00:19:37,130 --> 00:19:40,130
<b><i>Subtitles by JohnyX</b></i>

206
00:20:11,217 --> 00:20:14,217
Thank you.

207
00:21:32,555 --> 00:21:36,914
Mommy! Daddy!

208
00:21:39,115 --> 00:21:46,075
Hello, children.

209
00:21:46,914 --> 00:21:52,035
I'm your Auntie Neba. And you're Standa,
and you're Emilka, right?

210
00:21:53,515 --> 00:21:58,035
I arrived last night, and from now on,
I'll be staying here with you.

211
00:22:00,684 --> 00:22:03,684
I'll look after you so you
aren't all alone.

212
00:22:16,045 --> 00:22:23,144
And as soon as your parents return,
everything will be back to normal.

213
00:22:25,845 --> 00:22:30,731
I'll be kind to you. You have
nothing to fear.

214
00:23:08,101 --> 00:23:09,381
Goodbye, Auntie Neba.

215
00:23:25,520 --> 00:23:28,260
We'll be back right after school,
Auntie. Take care.

216
00:23:29,340 --> 00:23:33,441
And we aren't going anywhere, because
we have Auntie Neba.

217
00:23:44,766 --> 00:23:48,205
Can you believe it? Someone actually
turned up after all.

218
00:24:33,801 --> 00:24:36,221
That's good. Wait for your
parents at home.

219
00:25:38,242 --> 00:25:41,982
What are you doing here?

220
00:25:42,682 --> 00:25:44,163
We want to tidy up the garden.

221
00:25:44,843 --> 00:25:47,702
All right, I'll go get the shovels.

222
00:25:52,222 --> 00:25:55,222
Look!

223
00:25:59,823 --> 00:26:01,603
The snail is gone now.

224
00:26:02,503 --> 00:26:03,663
But he'll come back.

225
00:26:04,084 --> 00:26:08,403
No, he won't, Tomik. You see, he died.

226
00:26:09,663 --> 00:26:12,064
He won't come back today, but
maybe tomorrow...

227
00:26:12,884 --> 00:26:16,963
No, never. Never means forever.

228
00:26:17,443 --> 00:26:19,243
Exactly, Tomik.

229
00:26:20,203 --> 00:26:22,384
Then what's the point of his shell?

230
00:26:23,284 --> 00:26:29,683
It's a reminder that he existed. So
we can remember him.

231
00:26:37,622 --> 00:26:42,642
You kids go on ahead. I've got
things to do.

232
00:26:54,402 --> 00:26:55,981
Zuzka, will you tell me another story?

233
00:26:56,481 --> 00:26:58,122
Fine, I'll get the hat.

234
00:26:59,001 --> 00:27:01,682
Three ingredients.

235
00:27:02,376 --> 00:27:04,416
No, not another story.

236
00:27:05,576 --> 00:27:06,876
What's the matter?

237
00:27:07,356 --> 00:27:08,676
You don't have to listen.

238
00:27:15,517 --> 00:27:18,477
You can't even come up with a real
scary story anyway.

239
00:27:19,836 --> 00:27:22,696
Just you wait.

240
00:27:23,076 --> 00:27:25,356
I chose a spooky forest.

241
00:27:25,757 --> 00:27:28,017
And I chose a monstrous beast.

242
00:27:32,836 --> 00:27:36,296
Okay, third ingredient: an apple core.

243
00:27:38,135 --> 00:27:43,496
One, two, three.

244
00:27:43,875 --> 00:27:48,016
Once upon a time, there was a boy
named Jonas. On his way...

245
00:27:48,017 --> 00:27:52,155
to school every day, he passed
a mysterious gate.

246
00:28:08,662 --> 00:28:09,981
I told you it was locked.

247
00:28:11,922 --> 00:28:13,321
Here, help me.

248
00:28:16,102 --> 00:28:19,102
Careful.

249
00:28:31,922 --> 00:28:36,342
Come on, Jonas, jump!

250
00:28:40,201 --> 00:28:43,861
It's too high.

251
00:28:48,721 --> 00:28:51,721
Jump!

252
00:29:09,596 --> 00:29:11,696
Shouldn't we just go back?

253
00:29:12,136 --> 00:29:12,396
What?

254
00:29:13,057 --> 00:29:15,376
I'm not leaving without that
treasure you promised.

255
00:29:28,517 --> 00:29:31,416
But what if some unknown
monsters live there?

256
00:29:32,277 --> 00:29:33,876
I'll handle them, no problem.

257
00:29:56,222 --> 00:29:57,942
It's just like the jungle.

258
00:30:00,643 --> 00:30:03,643
Me!

259
00:30:07,363 --> 00:30:10,063
Hey, there was a snake!

260
00:30:10,462 --> 00:30:11,123
What?

261
00:30:11,442 --> 00:30:11,942
Probably a viper!

262
00:30:12,363 --> 00:30:12,982
Don't move!

263
00:30:15,002 --> 00:30:17,222
Stay still.

264
00:30:20,103 --> 00:30:25,843
A leaky hose. Very dangerous.

265
00:30:26,699 --> 00:30:28,439
But it was moving.

266
00:30:29,439 --> 00:30:30,179
Right.

267
00:30:30,979 --> 00:30:36,538
I see why the other guys don't
take you seriously.

268
00:30:52,118 --> 00:30:53,078
There's something in those bushes!

269
00:30:54,519 --> 00:30:56,259
Another snake?

270
00:31:11,596 --> 00:31:15,457
I thought you were just making it up
again, but this is actually cool.

271
00:31:18,396 --> 00:31:20,957
Yeah!

272
00:31:21,196 --> 00:31:22,217
Almost there!

273
00:31:28,968 --> 00:31:30,127
There are some boxes.

274
00:31:30,688 --> 00:31:31,788
Might be treasure inside.

275
00:31:42,428 --> 00:31:43,788
Maybe we shouldn't.

276
00:31:45,367 --> 00:31:46,428
Knock it off.

277
00:31:46,847 --> 00:31:48,488
No one's been here for years.

278
00:32:01,434 --> 00:32:02,654
I bet they're full of gold.

279
00:32:03,315 --> 00:32:04,535
We'll be rich!

280
00:32:17,795 --> 00:32:19,434
I can't move this by myself. Come help me.

281
00:32:33,480 --> 00:32:38,260
They're just old apple cores.

282
00:32:39,401 --> 00:32:41,060
They must be magical.

283
00:32:42,681 --> 00:32:43,681
Magical?

284
00:32:44,340 --> 00:32:46,280
Why else would anyone keep them?

285
00:32:47,020 --> 00:32:47,540
You know what?

286
00:32:48,621 --> 00:32:50,840
I've had enough of your stories.

287
00:32:51,980 --> 00:32:53,941
I'm going to play soccer with the boys.

288
00:32:55,240 --> 00:32:56,221
Wait!

289
00:32:58,520 --> 00:33:01,381
Vilda!

290
00:33:05,154 --> 00:33:08,154
Hello?

291
00:33:19,095 --> 00:33:22,095
Hello?

292
00:33:23,755 --> 00:33:26,134
Is anyone there?

293
00:33:59,375 --> 00:34:00,954
Yes? Well, come on in.

294
00:34:07,855 --> 00:34:09,574
We didn't mean to break in.

295
00:34:10,748 --> 00:34:14,807
You were looking for treasure and
found apple cores.

296
00:34:16,128 --> 00:34:20,588
And now you're curious why I save
them, aren't you?

297
00:34:24,148 --> 00:34:29,148
Go on, take a bite, and I'll tell you.

298
00:34:44,902 --> 00:34:45,943
This is amazing!

299
00:34:47,143 --> 00:34:55,663
When I was just a young girl, I
met a young man.

300
00:34:56,563 --> 00:34:59,342
Leontýnek Pokorný.

301
00:35:00,382 --> 00:35:02,822
He was very handsome.

302
00:35:04,362 --> 00:35:05,842
And an adventurer.

303
00:35:07,322 --> 00:35:12,844
He sailed the stormy seas and
trekked through

304
00:35:12,844 --> 00:35:18,967
the most treacherous jungles all
over the world.

305
00:35:19,987 --> 00:35:22,987
He was brave, too.

306
00:35:24,106 --> 00:35:26,286
I'm going to be an adventurer
one day, too.

307
00:35:26,887 --> 00:35:31,126
Really? But it's a dangerous life.

308
00:35:31,827 --> 00:35:32,746
I'm not afraid.

309
00:35:36,286 --> 00:35:36,806
Oops!

310
00:35:56,335 --> 00:36:01,255
But Leontýnek always came back to me
after every adventure.

311
00:36:02,034 --> 00:36:03,355
So we got married.

312
00:36:04,635 --> 00:36:12,715
As a wedding gift, he brought me a large
basket with a very strange creature inside.

313
00:36:13,675 --> 00:36:15,855
Neither tiger nor ostrich.

314
00:36:16,934 --> 00:36:18,175
Something in between.

315
00:36:18,974 --> 00:36:23,594
And it only ate apple cores.

316
00:36:24,554 --> 00:36:25,275
Apple cores?

317
00:36:25,833 --> 00:36:30,853
Well, so we planted apple trees.

318
00:36:31,773 --> 00:36:37,992
We ate nothing but apples and lived
happily together for many years.

319
00:36:39,612 --> 00:36:41,052
And what about the creature?

320
00:36:42,193 --> 00:36:44,833
It grew and grew.

321
00:36:53,221 --> 00:36:59,021
Eventually, it got too big for the
house, so we had to move it to the garden.

322
00:37:14,221 --> 00:37:17,581
But then, Leontýnek suddenly fell ill.

323
00:37:18,900 --> 00:37:22,561
It wasn't long before he was gone.

324
00:37:40,402 --> 00:37:45,143
Everything slowly became overgrown
and fell into ruin.

325
00:37:49,063 --> 00:37:55,123
And where did Leontýnek go that he
couldn't come back to help?

326
00:37:55,742 --> 00:37:58,103
Come, I'll show you.

327
00:38:13,967 --> 00:38:14,307
Okay.

328
00:38:16,068 --> 00:38:18,748
Wow, you have a real treasure trove here.

329
00:38:21,427 --> 00:38:23,288
But...

330
00:38:23,288 --> 00:38:28,188
Just junk that Leontýn brought
back from his travels.

331
00:38:30,947 --> 00:38:33,788
My real treasure is back there.

332
00:38:47,195 --> 00:38:53,114
My dear Leontýn Pokorný.

333
00:38:53,835 --> 00:39:02,614
He passed away, but we'll never be apart.

334
00:39:06,034 --> 00:39:12,715
He's the love of my life,
forever and always.

335
00:39:18,650 --> 00:39:20,630
I'm sorry, but I really have to run.

336
00:39:21,989 --> 00:39:24,710
But come back and help me with the
apple cores again.

337
00:39:25,210 --> 00:39:30,050
That beast eats so much, and I’m getting
a stomach ache from all these apples.

338
00:39:30,610 --> 00:39:32,710
I definitely will.

339
00:39:37,570 --> 00:39:39,769
Jonas?

340
00:39:40,329 --> 00:39:40,969
Yes.

341
00:39:46,527 --> 00:39:48,847
Take the key to the gate.

342
00:39:51,407 --> 00:39:54,407
Thank you.

343
00:39:57,427 --> 00:40:00,766
So you don't have to climb over the wall.

344
00:40:05,847 --> 00:40:11,186
But are you sure that beast isn't...

345
00:40:12,867 --> 00:40:14,646
dangerous?

346
00:40:47,242 --> 00:40:50,242
I'm...

347
00:40:57,262 --> 00:40:57,983
not afraid.

348
00:41:00,342 --> 00:41:03,342
Not...

349
00:41:06,483 --> 00:41:07,682
even a little bit.

350
00:42:04,789 --> 00:42:11,929
You like apples, don't you?

351
00:42:16,969 --> 00:42:18,739
Sweet, red ones.

352
00:42:28,610 --> 00:42:29,480
Yum, yum, yum, yum, yum!

353
00:42:56,309 --> 00:42:59,820
You liked that. I can give you
another bite.

354
00:43:05,190 --> 00:43:10,920
You’re not scary at all. No one
will ever believe this.

355
00:43:15,360 --> 00:43:17,639
Fairy, what are you doing there?

356
00:43:17,639 --> 00:43:19,920
I'm hiding from the monster.

357
00:43:24,090 --> 00:43:26,155
Her name is Beastie.

358
00:43:26,155 --> 00:43:28,320
Beastie, the monster... she
almost tore me apart.

359
00:43:28,590 --> 00:43:30,284
I think she just wanted to play.

360
00:43:30,284 --> 00:43:31,980
Wow, you're so brave.

361
00:43:32,429 --> 00:43:36,539
She nearly gobbled me up, and you tamed
her just like that. Hey!

362
00:43:37,769 --> 00:43:41,175
Luckily, she only eats apple cores.

363
00:43:41,175 --> 00:43:44,579
So you were right about them
being magical.

364
00:43:45,360 --> 00:43:47,460
We could come back and feed her tomorrow.

365
00:43:48,239 --> 00:43:50,400
Do you think she'd take a
core from me too?

366
00:43:50,400 --> 00:43:52,559
Well, maybe.

367
00:43:56,849 --> 00:43:59,429
Jonas, you'll stay with me just
in case, won't you?

368
00:43:59,429 --> 00:44:02,010
Sure, don't worry.

369
00:44:10,650 --> 00:44:12,119
She must be lonely here.

370
00:44:25,469 --> 00:44:26,010
Bye.

371
00:44:53,460 --> 00:44:56,610
And that's how Jonas tamed the monster.

372
00:44:56,610 --> 00:44:59,760
Yes, and all it took were some
old apple cores.

373
00:45:04,260 --> 00:45:07,769
Ow! Hey, watch it!

374
00:45:27,780 --> 00:45:28,469
Well.

375
00:45:35,550 --> 00:45:42,309
I can't get it to work. It's
just not working.

376
00:45:49,119 --> 00:45:54,226
That's Grandma's straw hat. I use
it for my stories.

377
00:45:55,686 --> 00:45:56,769
Bedtime stories?

378
00:45:58,059 --> 00:46:00,067
Yeah, her stories.

379
00:46:01,827 --> 00:46:04,947
Zuzka, I think Grandpa could use a story.

380
00:46:05,675 --> 00:46:09,070
A story? Me? No, no, no, no, no.

381
00:46:09,255 --> 00:46:11,735
It's getting cold and dark out.

382
00:46:12,954 --> 00:46:16,929
Come on, it's better inside.

383
00:46:20,920 --> 00:46:23,139
Wow, this is great! Look at that.

384
00:46:23,139 --> 00:46:25,360
Did you make this yourself?

385
00:46:25,389 --> 00:46:28,400
Grandpa helped me, but it's
not finished yet.

386
00:46:28,400 --> 00:46:29,409
Tom, stop it.

387
00:46:29,440 --> 00:46:31,715
Grandma would be proud of you.

388
00:46:35,375 --> 00:46:36,655
So, who's starting?

389
00:46:37,514 --> 00:46:37,875
Me!

390
00:46:38,594 --> 00:46:39,889
A colorful bird.

391
00:46:41,059 --> 00:46:41,534
A crown.

392
00:46:42,914 --> 00:46:44,815
Slippers?

393
00:46:47,054 --> 00:46:49,514
I couldn't think of anything else.

394
00:46:50,375 --> 00:46:50,960
It's fine.

395
00:46:54,559 --> 00:46:56,414
One, two, three.

396
00:46:56,815 --> 00:46:58,849
Once upon a time, there lived old Bohdan.

397
00:46:59,335 --> 00:47:02,574
The sun was rising, but Bohdan
was already awake.

398
00:48:05,132 --> 00:48:08,552
Three, two, one.

399
00:49:34,865 --> 00:49:38,047
Morning.

400
00:49:39,485 --> 00:49:45,666
Morning.

401
00:49:47,074 --> 00:49:49,275
Zaria, please take this over
to Mr. Bohdan.

402
00:49:49,335 --> 00:49:52,574
Here they are...

403
00:50:01,534 --> 00:50:04,594
One, four, nine, three sixty-seven.

404
00:50:05,235 --> 00:50:08,775
The papers have gone up a bit, you know.

405
00:50:09,675 --> 00:50:10,715
But not by much.

406
00:50:11,494 --> 00:50:12,914
Eight cents is still something.

407
00:50:13,454 --> 00:50:14,775
Eight here, eight there.

408
00:50:17,347 --> 00:50:19,208
No, no, no, I'll go back.

409
00:50:19,487 --> 00:50:21,188
It's fine.

410
00:50:21,447 --> 00:50:22,068
It can wait.

411
00:50:23,327 --> 00:50:24,327
No, really.

412
00:50:24,907 --> 00:50:25,887
I won't owe anything.

413
00:50:26,108 --> 00:50:29,027
But Mr. Bohdan, please, you
can pay tomorrow.

414
00:51:14,943 --> 00:51:18,222
It's only set to 18 now.

415
00:51:18,983 --> 00:51:20,242
Can you imagine?

416
00:51:28,882 --> 00:51:31,603
Anyone can do this nowadays.

417
00:51:34,523 --> 00:51:37,523
Yeah,

418
00:51:40,503 --> 00:51:41,503
they can.

419
00:51:57,315 --> 00:51:59,096
Etna.

420
00:52:01,735 --> 00:52:04,735
Do you remember

421
00:52:05,916 --> 00:52:07,456
Etna, Marti?

422
00:52:10,215 --> 00:52:18,056
Our first and last. Just our
first and last trip.

423
00:52:20,815 --> 00:52:22,596
But it wasn't because of me, you know.

424
00:52:25,327 --> 00:52:29,688
I was waiting for you to say you wanted
to go somewhere, but you never did.

425
00:52:31,047 --> 00:52:33,987
Why didn't you ever ask me to
take you somewhere?

426
00:52:35,007 --> 00:52:41,188
True, I should have offered it sooner.

427
00:52:47,027 --> 00:52:50,728
I'd have taken you around the world
three times, not just once.

428
00:52:51,208 --> 00:52:52,748
To hell with my rheumatism.

429
00:52:57,626 --> 00:52:59,086
Just so you know,

430
00:53:00,987 --> 00:53:02,327
I'd have even taken you by plane,

431
00:53:03,766 --> 00:53:05,166
just like you always wanted.

432
00:53:06,327 --> 00:53:07,987
Dammit!

433
00:53:07,987 --> 00:53:10,987
Blast it!

434
00:53:13,626 --> 00:53:14,746
And now this.

435
00:53:18,967 --> 00:53:23,306
Catch them for me!

436
00:53:27,530 --> 00:53:28,510
Catch them for me!

437
00:53:28,750 --> 00:53:30,769
I can't, it's too high.

438
00:53:32,309 --> 00:53:34,389
Jump, Mr. Bohdan, jump!

439
00:53:35,250 --> 00:53:36,590
Now, now, jump!

440
00:53:37,030 --> 00:53:37,610
Again!

441
00:53:46,230 --> 00:53:46,929
What's going on?

442
00:53:47,429 --> 00:53:48,050
Petr?

443
00:54:15,893 --> 00:54:18,893
Whoa!

444
00:54:31,873 --> 00:54:32,972
I'm flying.

445
00:54:40,873 --> 00:54:41,672
I'm flying.

446
00:54:44,175 --> 00:54:48,016
Eeny, meeny, cormorant, three little
pearls and five crows.

447
00:54:48,315 --> 00:54:52,356
Three little pearls and five crows,
and just one cormorant.

448
00:54:53,295 --> 00:55:01,335
Eeny, meeny, cormorant, three little
pearls and five crows.

449
00:55:02,735 --> 00:55:06,596
Eeny, meeny, cormorant, three little
pearls and five crows.

450
00:55:13,807 --> 00:55:15,768
I know, I know, I'm flying.

451
00:55:16,447 --> 00:55:18,467
I'm flying!

452
00:55:21,288 --> 00:55:24,288
Hey!

453
00:55:51,739 --> 00:55:53,918
But this is actually quite a workout.

454
00:56:02,579 --> 00:56:04,559
I haven't eaten much since this morning.

455
00:56:05,119 --> 00:56:07,679
I can't help it, I need to rest for a bit.

456
00:56:10,699 --> 00:56:15,239
But it's nothing,

457
00:56:16,159 --> 00:56:18,219
don't mention it.

458
00:56:23,130 --> 00:56:23,871
Thank you.

459
00:56:24,630 --> 00:56:26,331
Safe travels to you too.

460
00:57:07,646 --> 00:57:11,286
I'm here alone.

461
00:57:11,507 --> 00:57:12,586
All alone.

462
00:57:13,447 --> 00:57:14,567
I flew here.

463
00:57:15,507 --> 00:57:16,666
Can I explain?

464
00:57:19,666 --> 00:57:22,246
I flew with the cormorants for a bit.

465
00:57:23,306 --> 00:57:25,126
Cormorants, you know them?

466
00:57:25,827 --> 00:57:27,626
Well,

467
00:57:28,927 --> 00:57:35,490
I don't know. Just a moment ago I was
talking to Martinka,

468
00:57:36,090 --> 00:57:42,650
and then the wind suddenly picked up,
lifted me, and blew me all the way here.

469
00:57:43,030 --> 00:57:45,891
I don't really get how I ended
up here myself.

470
00:57:47,190 --> 00:57:49,990
Look, I'm just an ordinary old man.

471
00:57:51,550 --> 00:57:57,311
Let's put it this way, I don't want to
be arguing with birds at my age.

472
00:58:20,623 --> 00:58:21,422
My name is Bohdan.

473
00:58:21,882 --> 00:58:25,182
My mother was Marie and my
father was Radek.

474
00:58:29,760 --> 00:58:31,260
I've lost my Marta,

475
00:58:32,460 --> 00:58:35,061
but I'm sure she would have liked you.

476
00:59:51,875 --> 00:59:54,094
You're very kind.

477
00:59:54,514 --> 00:59:58,074
I hate to have put you to all
this trouble.

478
00:59:59,094 --> 01:00:06,554
One of you...

479
01:00:06,554 --> 01:00:08,135
was just like a bird.

480
01:01:46,523 --> 01:01:47,702
Three seventy-five.

481
01:01:48,543 --> 01:01:49,163
Four.

482
01:01:49,503 --> 01:01:50,682
Let's make it four.

483
01:01:51,963 --> 01:01:52,902
Thank you, Zaria.

484
01:01:54,443 --> 01:01:56,302
Have a lovely day, Ms. Aneta.

485
01:01:58,242 --> 01:02:00,503
You too, Mr. Bohdanec.

486
01:03:09,682 --> 01:03:15,282
That day, the old man decided he'd
never be a killjoy again.

487
01:03:30,262 --> 01:03:33,842
Thank you, sweetheart. Your story has
brought me joy again.

488
01:04:09,454 --> 01:04:11,574
Come on, kids, time to get up.

489
01:04:11,974 --> 01:04:15,574
All right, follow me.

490
01:04:18,875 --> 01:04:25,894
I'm not much of a storyteller, but I did
better with a few photos and mirrors.

491
01:04:27,135 --> 01:04:27,855
What's that?

492
01:04:40,740 --> 01:04:43,740
Grandma!

493
01:05:14,827 --> 01:05:15,967
It's beautiful.

494
01:05:21,068 --> 01:05:24,068
I'm glad.

495
01:05:36,507 --> 01:05:44,547
Grandpa, can you tell us your story?
It's the best birthday gift.

496
01:05:45,625 --> 01:05:48,625
You see,

497
01:05:59,905 --> 01:06:02,264
Grandma will never be far away.

498
01:06:03,364 --> 01:06:04,684
You're the best, Grandpa.

499
01:06:19,644 --> 01:06:21,824
Thank you.